{
The deliberation confirms that *southern*, *derivative*, *serenity*, and *gratitude* are standard examples of trisyllabic laxing. Between the remaining candidates, *pleasant* and *shadow*, the debate distinguishes between surface syllable count and historical vowel change. *Shadow* is the most phonologically sound answer because its Old English root vowel was inherently short, meaning it merely failed to undergo Middle English Open Syllable Lengthening rather than undergoing trisyllabic laxing of an underlying long vowel. Consequently, *shadow* never experienced trisyllabic laxing during its sound development.
}

